<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beneš decrees</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Beneš decrees were the laws issued by the Czechoslovak president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a> in exile and in 1945 before the parliament reconvened. They provided for the confiscation of property of Germans, Hungarians and collaborators and the loss of their citizenship, forming the legal basis of the expulsion of some three million Germans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benes-decrees.jpg" alt="Beneš decrees" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown. Transfer; United States. Office of War Information. Overseas Picture Division. Washington Division; 1944. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edvard_Bene%C5%A1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a>, elected president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1935, resigned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> in 1938. After the occupation he and other Czechoslovak politicians emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and in November 1939 set up the Czechoslovak National Committee. After the fall of France the committee moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where it became the Interim Czechoslovak Government, recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> on 21 July 1940.</p><p>The 1920 constitution gave the power to make laws to the National Assembly, which could not meet in exile, so the president's office was the only body with limited legislative power. Beneš returned to his post on the premise that his 1938 resignation under duress was invalid. Later decrees were proposed by the government in exile and co-signed by the prime minister or a minister, and their validity depended on ratification by the National Assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The decrees were issued from 21 July 1940 to 27 October 1945 and ratified by the Interim National Assembly on 6 March 1946. They covered the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>'s legal system, denazification and reconstruction. The term Beneš decrees is also used for the decrees and the ordinances of the Slovak National Council concerning ethnic Germans, Hungarians and others, which provided the legal framework for the expulsion of Germans.</p><p>Czech resistance groups, especially after Nazi reprisals for the assassination of Heydrich, demanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of ethnic Germans, and the government in exile sought Allied support for this from 1943. The decrees removed citizenship and confiscated the property of Germans and Hungarians, with exemptions for those who proved loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> or had been victims of Nazi or fascist terror. For Hungarians they also allowed dismissal of civil servants, banned the use of Hungarian in public offices and church services and the publication of Hungarian books, and excluded students from universities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The decrees are associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> in 1945–47 of about 3 million ethnic Germans and Hungarians. The relevant decrees omit any mention of deportation, whose basis was Article 12 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a>. About 250,000 Germans remained. A joint Czech-German commission found that over 20,000 German speakers died during the expulsion. A number of historians and legal scholars consider it ethnic cleansing.</p><p>The decrees were never repealed and remain controversial in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. In 1995 the Czech Constitutional Court held that a decree it reviewed was legitimate and had fulfilled its purpose. In 2005 the European Court of Human Rights refused the application of Josef Bergauer and 89 others. Slovakia's parliament adopted a resolution in 2007 rejecting revision of postwar decisions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the Czech Republic signed a declaration of mutual apology in 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beneš decrees” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The post-war expulsion of about three million Germans under the <b>Beneš decrees</b> ended centuries of Czech-German coexistence in Bohemia and Moravia and remains a sensitive subject.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
